2 SURN Leventhal
2 _MARNM Leventhal
1 SEX F
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} In an attempt to save his young children, Chaya Sarah and Moshe Yehuda, Rabbi Yissachar handed them over to a family of Polish gentiles to be kept safe until the end of the war. 
2 CONT After the war, Chaya Sarah continued to grow up with her Polish adoptive mother, as her parents had perished and did not return to claim her. Her adoptive parents concealed the fact that she was Jewish from her. More than fifty ye
2 CONC ars later, as her adoptive mother lay on her deathbed, she revealed to her daughter the secret that had been hidden all those years: that she was Jewish, the daughter of Rabbi Yissachar and Tziril Freida Lowenthal. She further tol
2 CONC d her that her father had been the town’s rabbi and that his family was a prominent rabbinic lineage, serving in various towns in the surrounding region.
2 CONT
2 CONT The woman was shaken by the revelation and immediately began investigating whether any of her relatives remained. In 1996, she succeeded in contacting her cousins in New York and learned further details about her family and her pa
2 CONC st. Consequently, she immigrated to Israel, returned to her father’s heritage, and began observing the Torah and Mitzvos.
2 CONT
2 CONT The traces of her brother, Moshe Yehuda, were lost, and his fate remains unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: The Rav of Furth, p. 90; Shevach Ahuvim, pp. 169–170.
