1 NAME Chanoch Henoch /Bornstein/ 4th Sochatchover Rebbe
2 GIVN Chanoch Henoch
2 SURN Bornstein
2 NSFX 4th Sochatchover Rebbe
2 _MARNM Bornstein
2 NICK Hanoch
2 NICK Bornsztain
1 NAME חנוך העניך /ברנשטיין/
2 GIVN חנוך העניך
2 SURN ברנשטיין
2 _MARNM ברנשטיין
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 21 OCT 1896
2 PLAC Sochaczew, Poland
2 ADDR
1 BURI
2 DATE SEP 1965
2 PLAC Har HaMenuchot, Jerusalem, Israel
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 23 SEP 1965
2 PLAC Jerusalem, Israel
2 ADDR
1 OCCU Rebbe
1 TITL Rabbi
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chanoch_Henoch_Bornsztain
2 CONT
2 CONT Chanoch was the second son of Rabbi Shmuel Bornsztain, the second Sochatchover Rebbe, and his wife, Yuta Leah. He grew up in the presence of his illustrious grandfather, Rabbi Avrohom Bornsztain, also known as the Avnei Nezer, who
2 CONC  founded the Sochatchover dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1924, Rabbi Chanoch accompanied his brother Rabbi Dovid to Palestine for the purpose of purchasing land. Rabbi Dovid put a down payment on a site south of Ramla. Upon his return to Poland, however, his efforts to collect the re
2 CONC mainder of the money were stymied by Polish laws, and he lost his down payment as well as the right to the land.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, Rabbi Chanoch stayed in Jerusalem, living first in the Bucharim neighborhood and then settling in the new Bayit VeGan neighborhood, where he established a beth midrash (study hall).
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the death of their father in 1926, Rabbi Dovid acceded to the leadership of the Sochatchover Hasidim, while Rabbi Chanoch continued to live and study in Palestine.
2 CONT
2 CONT After World War II, when Rabbi Chanoch heard about the death of his brother in the Warsaw Ghetto, he was inconsolable. Later, he accepted the request of the Sochatchover Hasidim to be their leader. He established his court in Jeru
2 CONC salem, preferring to remain in that city even during the 1948 Arab-Israeli war, when his followers in Tel Aviv urged him to join him there.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi Chanoch led the Hasidut until his death on 23 September 1965 (26 Elul 5725). He is buried on Har HaMenuchot in Jerusalem.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son, Rabbi Menachem Shlomo Bornsztain, succeeded him as Rebbe.
