1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From: Victor Shields [rhovic@rogers.com]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sent: March 2, 2007 10:10 AM
2 CONT
2 CONT To: Terri Wiedre; elise wiedre; home Victor; Jean Sutkiewicz; Robin Shields; Jamie Shields; David Shields; Evelyn Gold
2 CONT
2 CONT Subject: Rabbi Silverstein's saga.doc
2 CONT
2 CONT Attachments: header.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT April 21, 2003
2 CONT
2 CONT Dear Victor and Riki:
2 CONT
2 CONT First I hope that this finds you both recovered from planning and arranging the wedding. I know what it is like. Also I wish to say that I and Gayle were glad that we attended. It was a beautiful wedding in every way.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also we enjoyed meeting the children and grand- children, the new members of the Sheilds and Rabbi Silverstein clan.
2 CONT
2 CONT As for me and Gayle we are back home. We had no problems with flight arrangements. In fact we caught an earlier flight, and in an hour and a half we were back in Chicago. If it's any consolation. the weather in Chicago was even wo
2 CONC rse that the one in Toronto. Chicago had lots of snow and slush and the first thing I had to do when I got to my house was the clean-off 6­inches of snow from my car.
2 CONT
2 CONT Victor, as you requested, I am sending you my memoir of the "Rabbi Silverstein's coming to Toronto from Poland". I hope you may find it interesting. Have a happy and healthy Passover.
2 CONT
2 CONT Love Morris
2 CONT
2 CONT September 21, 1995
2 CONT
2 CONT Dear Frimette: Over the phone you asked me to write about the memories I may recall, that I have from the time when the family came to Toronto and even prior to that when we still lived in Poland. You asked me to make a tape and s
2 CONC end it to you. I figured that since I work around a lot of computers why not type my memories and send you a print-out. So I have started on it. I hope that you will overlook if I make some grammatical mistakes and perhaps make so
2 CONC me spelling errors, or make mistakes about the actual happenings at that time or about the characters involved. So here goes.
2 CONT
2 CONT I must have been about 3 years old when the family moved from Bodzentyn to a town called Wierzbnik. Bodzentyn or "Baizetcheen" as the Jewish folks called it had burnt down. So my father got a new position as the main rabbi in Wier
2 CONC zbnik.  At that age I remember only one thing; that we all got on a large horse-­driven wagon, where we had piled our furniture and belongings and we all got on the wagon atop the furniture and clothing to move to Wierzbnik.
2 CONT
2 CONT Along the way somehow there was an accident. The wagon overturned into a ditch along the unpaved road and we all got spilled out with the furniture. Luckily no one was injured as the wagon was not travelling too fast. I cried (aft
2 CONC er all I was still a baby) and I remember it was a sunny day, and your mother, Frimette, dried my tears and calmed me. One thing I want you to know. That whatever I'm describing while in my child formative years, at that time I di
2 CONC dn't think or reason about what was happening after the occurrence. It was later as a teen-ager and young adult that these memories came back to me and as an adult I placed them into focus as to what they were.
2 CONT
2 CONT We moved to Wierzbnick, a larger town that I later learned was a manufacturing city, some in producing steel. First we lived in what I now feel was a dump.  It was an old house which I didn't like much. Later we moved in a newer a
2 CONC partment building owned by a Mr. Rotenberg, who had a daughter called Sosha who became your mother's good friend. She is in one of the pictures you have of your mother and her girl friends.
2 CONT
2 CONT We lived in that apartment until we went to Canada. It was a nice building but it had no amenities.  The outhouse was out in the back quite a distance. The landlord had quite a large pool in the back of the building, where he trie
2 CONC d to breed fish.                                   There was no running water.  We had to bring up pails of water to fill a barrel that stood out in the corridor or hall. Your mother and my sister Esther- were usually the ones who
2 CONC  used to fill the barrels.
2 CONT
2 CONT Now my father was well-liked in his post as rabbi in Wierzbnick, The stipend was not too great. My mother wore a key around her middle on some sort of a girdle. That key was for the "Shunk," where she locked up the daily few loave
2 CONC s of breed that was purchased from the bakery.  Sometimes they purchased a few bagels, which tasted like heaven to me, when and if they gave me a piece.
2 CONT
2 CONT Every day or near mealtime she would unlock the "Shunk" take out the bread cut into thick slices and give each of us the piece of bread. I believe most of the food we lived on was potatoes prepared in every form possible. Of cours
2 CONC e when your mother fried latkes it was a real treat.
2 CONT
2 CONT Now my father was worried about his marriageable daughters. He knew that without a dowry they didn't have a chance of marrying. At that time, he had written a book a "Sayfer". He had been receiving letters while still living in Bo
2 CONC dzentyn from some of the people who had lived in Bodzentyn and gone to Canada. Yosel Gewertz and others like him used to write him and maybe send a few dollars from time to time.  These ex-Bodzentynians grew up while my father was
2 CONC  serving in Bodzentyn and were very, very fond of him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Evidently my father must have learned from some friends or rabbis that he corresponded with, that those who had composed a "Sayfer" went to America or Canada and there, the good religious Jewish men would buy his book and thus rai
2 CONC se some money. Frankly, in retrospect, I admire him very much that he had the guts to go out into a strange world to try to raise money for his daughters' dowries: His  great Jewish ethic to see daughters married off drove him to 
2 CONC this unorthodox action. If not for his action, none of us or the uncle Solomon's family would be here.
2 CONT
2 CONT So my father went to Canada to Toronto. He was met by Yosel Gewertz and a few other men, disciples from Bodzentyn at Union Railway Station, and installed him in the home of one of them, a nice Jewish family, that my father knew an
2 CONC d was reliable as to kashruth. They gave up their double parlor to him. The dining room became his bedroom and the living room became his study. The lady of the house and her husband were thrilled to have him. To them it was a gre
2 CONC at honor. It was like having a cardinal from Rome staying with them. They treated him royally and took great care of him in every way. I remember my father at one time mention this. As a boy I had big ears, I listened to everythin
2 CONC g that people said.
2 CONT
2 CONT During that time my father reached the zenith of his career.  The whole Jewish community was agog at his arrival and they all came to see him. He conducted sermons in the big Poilisha Shule, the London shule on Spadina, and all th
2 CONC e little branch shules on different Saturdays and holidays. The branch shules were the Keltzer, the Slipia, and the one on Dundas near Beverley St. (I don't recall it's name just now) and a few other small shules named after the t
2 CONC owns the members came from. For these little branch shules your father used to print their letterheads and envelopes. 
2 CONT
2 CONT My father was very popular. The Jewish men were constant visitors to my father in his study. Most of them gave him monetary gifts. Yosal Gewertz and Yosela Rothstein were his constant visitors and advisors.
2 CONT
2 CONT They practically lived there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Now my father's idea in coming to Toronto was to raise a few thousand dollars for the dowries and go back home to his family in Wierzbnik. In Toronto he was preparing himself to return after he had been there 6 months. Then Yosel 
2 CONC Gewertz, and Yosela Rothstein and the presidents of the small branch shules that he went to each on different Saturdays to hold a sermon, began to say to him not to go home, but to decide to stay permanently in Toronto. They knew 
2 CONC how to convince him. They said to him that he would do a mitzvah to the Jewish community. That the Jewish people needed to have someone like my father to bring more Jewishkeit  into the lives of the Jewish community.
2 CONT
2 CONT They further said to him: Why do you want to go back to such an anti-Semitisha country like Poland. What kind of future will your children have over there. Your daughters even if you married them off, what would their husbands do 
2 CONC to make a living ?   Anyways they finally convinced him.  They told him that they would arrange the financial part.     They will buy the steamship tickets and they will rent a house and furnish it for his family. He wouldn't have
2 CONC  to lift a finger.  What convinced him even more was his great popularity. At all the services where he preached the shule was packed. He really felt that it would be for the good of Yiddishkeit to stay. So he said yes to his (I�1
2 CONC 1 call them chasidim) friends.
2 CONT
2 CONT My father wrote home to Wierzbnik to tell us the good news that he was bringing over the family to Toronto. Also to tell little Moishe Yechiel that when he gets to Toronto he will have plenty of milk to drink, (as I liked milk of 
2 CONC which we could little afford to buy). I was the bread and milk kid.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our family consisted of the following. Gitel and her family. They lived in Radom.     Her husband with his family was to take over my father's post in Wierzbnik, and move in to the apartment we had occupied. For that time and plac
2 CONC e the apartment was rather nice as it was a new building only a few years old. Oh yes. When my father went to Canada for a 6 month visit, he had installed Gitel's husband Benzion to serve in my father's place. He lived with us, wh
2 CONC ile Gitel and her children remained in Radom with her in-laws.  When my father decided to stay in Canada, he wrote to the powers to be in the Wierzbnik Jewish Community and arranged for Benzion to be installed as the permanent rab
2 CONC bi of Wierzbnik. They had gotten used to Benzion while he was subbing for my father.  So that was taken care of.
2 CONT
2 CONT The rest of the family consisted of as follows:  Shaindel, she was a divorcee with a different name.  My father wrote that she should stay with Gitel and that she would be brought over a few months later. As they needed to work sp
2 CONC ecial to get a visa for her.
2 CONT
2 CONT The ones to come were, my mother, Dinah, Esther, Edith, Al, Harry, and yours truly. We began to get ready. My father sent money for us to buy clothing and whatever was necessary for the trip. As a kid I could feel the excitement t
2 CONC he family felt. I remember they made me a little beige colored winter coat, and they got me new shoes. I was terribly proud of my coat and strutted around in it. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally the day came and we got on a train to go to Warsaw. in Warsaw we were to go to the Canadian Consul and get our visas. We had made arrangements to stay with our Aunt Riva, my mother's youngest sister.
2 CONT
2 CONT I don't recall if anyone met us or didn't meet us at the Warsaw Station. But I remember being at Aunt Rivals home. What a difference. My Aunt Riva was a. very lovely woman, highly educated and with class. She was married to Melech
2 CONC  Elblinger.                            They were very well to do and perhaps considered quite wealthy. They had 3 sons all handsome and 2 daughters very nice looking. Aunt Riya and her family were very modern for those times and P
2 CONC olishized themselves in their way of life. All their children went to the "gymnasium" pronounced with a hard "G" in other words university.
2 CONT
2 CONT The oldest son at that time had gone to Palestine. He was an agronomist and worked in Palestine and Egypt. The other 2 sons still lived in Warsaw and attended the university. The oldest daughter her name was Gela also attended the
2 CONC  university. The youngest daughter about 15, attended high school. Try to remember that at that time I heard them talk but didn't realize what it meant. It was later as an adult I understood what they were talking about.
2 CONT
2 CONT My Aunt Riva was very kind to us. She made us welcome. They owned a complex which consisted of a courtyard and when you entered the front gate you saw a courtyard and a big building shaped like a "U" with two levels. The two level
2 CONC s were divided into apartmentsi it was gallery style with walkways in front of the apartments. Modern motels in the south use this style.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aunt Riva let us use an apartment that must have been vacant at that time. We were to stay about a week in Warsaw. I believe she and perhaps the people from the steamship line who had representatives in Warsaw helped us to get the
2 CONC  Visas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aunt Riva�s apartment was gorgeous. It was just like a beautiful French Style apartment, like in the movie "A Song To Remember" with Merle Oberon and Cornel Wilde. It is still possible to rent a video of A Song To Re­member. They 
2 CONC had gold drapes, carpeting and beautiful divans. They had flush toilets which was wonderful to me. I kept playing with the flushing mechanism I pulled the chain and it would flush. My aunt caught me doing it and kindly asked me no
2 CONC t to as might spoil it.
2 CONT
2 CONT One day I wandered into my Aunt's apartment and I didn't get too far but I got into her eldest daughter Gela's room. It was a beautifully furnished room with drapes in a gold color, beautiful furniture, carpeting and a dressing mi
2 CONC rror. It was her boudoir.  She gave me a dirty look, and said in Polish harshly and pointed to me to get out. She looked at me as if I was dirt. My "peyis" and my country style clothing didn't help. Later in life I realized that t
2 CONC hey were trying to get into the high class Polish mainstream culture and trying to be accepted and not to be considered �A parshiva Jid" if you know what I mean. I left the room quickly and I made up my mind that I didn't like sno
2 CONC tty Gela.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sons of Aunt Riva and the youngest daughter were very nice and courteous to me. While on the subject of Aunt Riva�s children, Gela was caught with her parents in the holocaust. The young daughter escaped to Switzerland, and th
2 CONC e sons escaped to Palestine where when I was there with my parents we visited them. They invited us to their home in Tel Aviv, and visited them in their bicycle store which the two younger brothers operated there. They were very c
2 CONC ourteous to us.
2 CONT
2 CONT Going back to Aunt Riva. She was a lovely, good-looking, slim woman, educated, well dressed, and kind hostess. They had servants.  The best part for me was her husband, Melech Eiblinger. He was tall well-built and very handsome. H
2 CONC e had a jewelry and combination pawnshop business. We immediately took to each other. He talked to me as if I was a grown up. On a Saturday afternoon I think he took me for a walk. We walked the streets of Warsaw, he explained to 
2 CONC me what things were, and I remember he said to me come and I will show you the "Halle". Actually it was that era's style of a shopping mail. It was patterned after the "Halle" in Paris, France which I understand was just recently 
2 CONC torn down and replaced with a modern mall. The best part of that walk was that he held my little hand in his strong palm.  I don't know why but this little action stuck in my mind. He was dressed Prince Albert style if you know wh
2 CONC at I mean, very distinguished. I guess he liked me because I was a little 5 year old boy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT We finally left Warsaw to go to what you might call an immigrant processing center. It was run by either the steamship line we were going on the Cunard White Star Line, or it may have been run by the American and Canadian governme
2 CONC nts in conjunction to process immigrants about to emmigrate to the US. and Canada.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was a town called Veycherova not far from Danzig where we were to embark on the ship. When we got to Veycherova we got into a center where there was a part for men and a part for women.  They made us go thru showers, took our c
2 CONC lothes to be deloused whether you had lice or not, and then we went thru a physical exam by doctors.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the physical exam they stamped the visa yes or no I assume, and they gave us back our clothes. They must have steamed the clothing and they shrunk everyones� clothes. They barely fit. I cried because my beautiful new coat wa
2 CONC s tight and the fabric had lost it's sheen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Now we come to a little bad part. Dinah my sister was not OK'd because of her eyes, and my sister Esther was refused because of her legs. We were terribly upset about this. But either the ship people or the HIAS, the Jewish Organi
2 CONC zation people who looked out for Jewish immigrants told us not to lose hope but that they will try to get them to OK the visa s later on. That it will take a few months.   In the meantime, they said that Dinah and Esther will have
2 CONC  to go back to Wierzbnik to stay with Gitel until the papers for them will be OK'd. They would see that they got to Wierzbnik alright.
2 CONT
2 CONT We had no choice so my mother, your mother Edith, Al, Harry and I got on a train that took us to Danzig the port city which wasn't far from Veychorova and got on the ship, which took us to Southampton, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Southampton we took a train to London. Either the steamship people or the HIAS guided us to a house that put up transient immigrants for a few days.
2 CONT
2 CONT The house for immigrants was very nice clean and bright accommodation. We had nice rooms, soft beds, bathrooms, and they served us meals.    I assume that the food was kosher.
2 CONT
2 CONT We stayed there I think a day and a half and then they (the immigrant guides) took us to the train to go to Liverpool where we were to embark on a ship that   would take us to Canada.
2 CONT
2 CONT If I remember correctly the name of the ship was the Montcalm. It was a pretty large ship owned by the Cunard White Star Line. We had quarters what might be called "B" tourist class. It wasn�t too bad. We had two cabins. The food 
2 CONC wasn't bad either but neither was it high class. Anyways I don't remember how many days we spent on the ship, but eventually we arrived in Halifax. We disembarked and were put into a processing center that had beds as well, for th
2 CONC ose who were not processed immediately or had to wait for trains to take them to there destination.
2 CONT
2 CONT I'm not a 100% sure, but I think we ran into some trouble with the immigration department. They wouldn't admit my brother Harry because of an eye problem. The HIAS helpers told us that they will straighten it out and that everythi
2 CONC ng will be OK, but that Al and I will have to leave on the train, and that my mother and Edith and Harry will remain, and in a day or two they will follow us to Toronto. So we got to Toronto. I must have fallen asleep because I do
2 CONC n't remember arriving in Toronto. When I woke up I was in a house which I later found out was 54 Cecil Street, and that it was to be our home. In retrospect, I believe that the ship people or the HIAS people made Al and I leave be
2 CONC cause it would have been an extra expense to keep two immigrants in the processing center, to house them and feed them.
2 CONT
2 CONT in about a couple of days my mother, Edith and Harry did arrive in Toronto and settled into 54 Cecil Street. I will describe the house later, but now I would like to say, that Dinah and Esther did come maybe G months later, father
2 CONC 's friends worked it out somehow. Shaindel was brought over about a year later. By right I should end this history of the family's coming to Canada now, as this story finally brought the family to Toronto. However I hope the reade
2 CONC r will humor me and let me write about the house we began to live in that is 54 Cecil Street. You know this would make a good title for a book "54 Cecil Street".
2 CONT
2 CONT As a little boy of about 6 years old I would listen-in to the elder people's talk. I gathered that after my father decided to remain in Toronto and bring over his family, his friends said to my father, Rabbi we will start looking 
2 CONC for a house to rent and get it ready for your family when they arrive.
2 CONT
2 CONT They found this house on 54 Cecil Street, which was in the center of the Jewish community and near everything. My father liked the house and they rented it. By the way the landlord of the house was a Mr. Steinwurtzel, a wealthy ma
2 CONC n, in the clothing business, who also lived on Cecil Street and was also one of my father's chassidim. The Steinwurtzel family eventually built and owned the famous Tip Top Tailors factory.
2 CONT
2 CONT My father's friends took him shopping for furniture to furnish the house. My father, Rabbi Silverstein wasn't just a Rabbi wrapped up in his books.     He was an intelligent and worldly man who knew human nature and what went on i
2 CONC n the world. He knew how to travel on trains, and I remember when he took me along on his travels, he told me that we must always tip the waiters and porters. My father paid for the furniture.
2 CONT
2 CONT Now back to 54 Cecil street. When I saw the house I fell in love with it. The house was a red brick building Victorian style, in which a Doctor had lived. It had 4 levels.  A high English basement that had plastered walls and ceil
2 CONC ings and wooden flooring and a toilet and sink.
2 CONT
2 CONT The main floor had the double parlor, dinning room and a large kitchen. The -kitchen had a narrow style gas range and a separate large wood-burning stove with the name Happy Thought on it.     The large stove was trimmed with shin
2 CONC y nickel.   My mother loved that stove more than the gas stove.
2 CONT
2 CONT The front hallway had a grand staircase going up with a shiny mahogany banister, but the staircase I loved was the little twisted staircase that ran up from the kitchen to the second floor, which was probably built for the servant
2 CONC s use. The second floor had the bedrooms, the main bathroom. a sunroom with window doors that opened. Then there was the attic with 4 bedrooms with slanting ceilings. The rooms were quite roomy probably meant for the servants. I f
2 CONC ell in love with one room the one at the south-east corner, because it had a balcony, that you could walk onto. The balcony was under a pointed gable that was built jutting out from the building. I immediately claimed the room wit
2 CONC h the balcony for myself.
2 CONT
2 CONT When we moved into the house, we were 6 people. My father, my mother, Edith, Al, Harry and myself. To go back a little. I learned that once the house was rented, my father's friends and their wives got together to make the house l
2 CONC ivable. They had the house cleaned and decorated. Then the women folk of my father's friends, led by : Mrs. Goldhar, Mrs Goldala Goldberg, Mrs. Yosela Rothstein, and Mrs. Zarnitzky, got together many women and they fixed up the ho
2 CONC use from top to bottom.
2 CONT
2 CONT There were lace curtains on the windows. The beds were made up with linen, pillows and blankets. The kitchen was fixed up with dinnerware, cutlery in all the drawers, pots and pans. There was food of all sorts.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the basement there was a sack of flour in a tin lid­ded covered barrel, sack of potatoes, sack of onions, a wooden crate of eggs, oranges, bushel of apples and upstairs in the double sided ice box there were chickens and meats.
2 CONC  We walked into the house and my mother and Edith didn't have to lift a finger. it was like magic. The house was bright, clean and sparkling. That is what my father's friends did because they were so fond of him and respected him.
2 CONC  We came to Toronto about 2 weeks before passover, I think the year was 1925 in April, and that was one of the reasons the ladies fixed up the house so completely. The men and their wives, my father's friends paid for the fixing u
2 CONC p of the house and for all the food.
2 CONT
2 CONT My mother being of a delicate health Edith took over, the running of the house. The menfolk and the women­folk fell in love with Edith. Edith was young and slim and very pretty, with dark curly hair and sparkling eyes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of course my father had many friends. The two closest of the friends were Yosel Gewertz and Yosela Rothstein. They were in and out of the house constantly, to see if they could be of any help, and they were of much help. Yosela Ro
2 CONC thstein brought along one of his sons, a very good-looking son whose name was Mathew. In Yiddish he was "Maates", to help my father out. He was Canadian born. He was about 19 or 20 years old. He did help my father out a lot. He sp
2 CONC oke Yiddish and English. He ran errands, helped move my father's books, went to the 
2 CONT
2 CONT bank for him, paid the utility bills at the bank and many, many other household things that needed fixing. He went to the Post Office, etc. etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT Well when we all came, and he saw Edith, he fell in love with her. He talked to her, he helped her with whatever she was doing. He tried to teach her English. He hung around the house as soon as the sun rose, and stayed around til
2 CONC l late in the evening. He brought his wind-up victrola, played records and would sing along to her with the record that was playing. I remember one record that he played constantly. The opening line was "Your lovely to look at and
2 CONC  heaven to hold" etc. Since I didn't speak English it sounded to me like Your lovely Tolucca, and I thought it was a funny name. He stuck around the house so late, that my father yelled at him "Gey shoin aheim Maates, es is shpeyt
2 CONC ". He was obsessed with Edith.
2 CONT
2 CONT When your mother became engaged to Louis Shields, he was terribly broken-hearted and he cried. Yosela Rothstein came to my father and begged him to break off Edith's engagement and have her marry Mathew. My father declined, and af
2 CONC ter that the Rothsteins stopped coming to our house and the friendship cooled off a lot. However about 6 or 7 months later they became friendly again.  Your mother was sad about this too and she cried.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the meantime, my father arranged for Al to go to a Yeshiva in New York City. For my brother Harry and and myself he enrolled us with a Malamed, on Beverly street. I only remember the malamed's first name, Aaron, to tutor us. He
2 CONC  did not want us to go to a goyisha public school.
2 CONT
2 CONT However Yosel Gewertz (I believe it was ) had pity on us and informed the powers to be at the Board of Education that there were two boys that were not attending public school. So one fine early morning while my father was in shul
2 CONC e davening, There came
2 CONT
2 CONT a truant officer to the house and routed us out of bed and made us get dressed and took us off and enrolled both of us in the public school on Nassau Street a
2 CONT
2 CONT a little west of Spadina Aye.
2 CONT
2 CONT I loved the school from the start. There was nothing my father could do to stop our going to school. People who came from Europe had a high respect and fear of the goyisha government. So we continued to go to public school, and in
2 CONC  the afternoons we went to cheder.
2 CONT
2 CONT I think I will stop here. I hope Frimette that this memoir will help you in your research about the family's coming to Canada.
2 CONT
2 CONT Morris
2 CONT
2 CONT
